description: Each lane's row names its own corpus dirs: node's rows list theirs in items.tsv, P4.5 runs corpus/wasm and corpus/scalar for wasmtime (#74), and the llvm lane repeats the node list by hand in checks/common-corpora.sh. A dir added to one lane's row is silently skipped by the others. And the evidence for a change is a row and its exit plus a prose note, so nothing records "this program: native MATCH, wasm MATCH, llvm MISMATCH".
 
 I propose:
 1. One list of the common program corpora (a tracked file) that every lane's corpus row reads, instead of naming dirs itself.
 2. A backend registry: each backend's WASM_RUN runner, its probe, and whether it is required. Required now: native lg (its .expected snapshot), WasmGC on node, llvm on the host profile. Recorded when present: llvm fix31, wasmtime, the linear target, self-compiled and ORC; a backend becomes required when its owner declares it supported. Required is the LW_REQUIRE_<runtime> switch git-issue:f1a846c proposes, D227's shape.
 3. run-corpus.sh emits one cell per program per backend: MATCH, MISMATCH, REFUSED, SKIP <reason>, NOT BUILT. Cells go in the row's TAP YAML block (D229) and in a keyed per-machine ledger beside gate-times.tsv: program, backend, input key (attest.sh's), result.
 4. pr-ready and the gate refuse when a required backend's cell for a common program is missing, or is neither MATCH nor a refusal the program declares (`;; refuse-<backend>: <text>`) with the backend refusing in that text.
 
 The one change to your lanes is item 1: the node and wasmtime rows read their dirs from the list instead of naming them. Their ids, runners and SKIP behavior stay.
 
 Facts that close it:
 - F1 the lane rows for node, wasmtime and llvm read one list: grep of items.tsv names no corpus dir in those rows.
 - F2 a row's TAP block carries one cell per program per backend: checks/gate.sh output parsed by P0.7.
 - F3 a required cell missing or failing makes pr-ready and gate exit non-zero: a self-test that drops one cell.
 - F4 checks/common-corpora.sh is gone or reads the list.
 
 Open questions:
 - The list's home: corpus/common.txt, or a column in corpus-budget.tsv?
 - Does the ledger travel (a git note per commit, as evidence does), or stay per machine as gate-times.tsv does?
 
 Refs git-issue:f1a846c (LW_REQUIRE_<runtime>), git-issue:907a973 (known-failing rows), git-issue:7e15c35 (a row that skips one lane reports a plain ok), git-issue:f650769 (llvm parity).

## Comments
[2026-10-30T19:07:49Z] Matt Parrett: Yes to the shape, with one condition on item 4 and answers to the two questions.
 
 Item 1 now: the node and wasmtime rows read the list; a tracked corpus/common.txt, one dir per line, with the lane refusals staying in the programs' headers. corpus-budget.tsv is about sizes and would make a dir's membership a side effect of its budget row.
 
 Items 2 and 3 as proposed: the registry, the cells in the TAP block, a per-machine ledger beside gate-times.tsv. The ledger stays per machine; the cells that matter travel already, inside the TAP block the evidence note carries, so a second travelling copy would be two sources for one fact.
 
 Item 4 with the required set declared in the registry per branch, not fixed in the proposal. On main today the required backends are native and WasmGC on node; llvm joins when the llvm target lands on main. Until then a GC-lane PR cannot produce an llvm cell on main's tree, so the refusal would fire on every one of them. Cost is the other half of the condition. A required llvm cell means every PR touching a common program compiles it on the llvm lane: seconds per program warm, half a minute for the world dump. The gate's row times (D229) will show whether that fits the budget or needs the function cache first.
 
 Facts that close it hold as written. F3's self-test should drop a cell for each required backend in turn, so a registry edit cannot silently make one optional.
